\DOC LOCAL_CMSUB41_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_CMSUB41_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
LOCAL_CMSUB41_P521_TAC advances a symbolic-execution {eventually} goal past an
inlined modular combine-and-subtract subroutine, replacing the current state
variable by a later one and adding an assumption characterising the newly
written result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting which code/offset variant of the block
is stepped over, which affects the program-counter value and the register/stack
layout assumed; the second is a {string list} naming the result variable and
the two input variables whose relationship is asserted in the new assumption.
In the conclusion {eventually x86 (\s'. ...) s}, the state variable {s} is
replaced by a fresh, later state variable {s'}-successor, the postcondition
predicate itself being left unchanged. All assumptions mentioning the old state
— the program counter, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame — are discharged and re-added for the new state,
with the program counter updated to the block's exit address, the {MAYCHANGE}
frame extended by the newly written destination location, and a new memory-read
assumption equating the destination to the named result variable; additionally
a pure arithmetic assumption of the form {t ==> u}, relating the named result
to the named inputs modulo the prime, is added, and some unchanged assumptions
are renumbered.
{
   A(s) ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s
   ======================================  LOCAL_CMSUB41_P521_TAC n ["r"; "a"; "b"]
   A'(z1), t ==> u ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') z1
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, on goals of
this one symbolic-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
